# COLOURS THE BROWSER OWNS, NOT THE PALETTE.
#
# The colour inventory (ADR-0110) maps every rendered colour to one of the app's
# seven roles, so any palette can be checked by arithmetic. A form control the
# app never colours is painted by the USER AGENT from `color-scheme`, so no role
# produces it and no palette can change it.
#
# That is not automatically wrong — a native control that follows the platform is
# often the right answer, and since 3.3.0 it follows the READER'S choice rather
# than the device's. But it must be a decision somebody made, not a gap, because
# a control the palette cannot reach will look like the platform in every palette
# and like the product in none.
#
# Held in BOTH directions: an undeclared UA colour fails the extraction, and a
# declaration whose selector no longer renders one fails it too — so an exemption
# cannot outlive the thing it exempts.
#
#   <selector> | <why>
#
#journal-text | a <textarea>: the app styles its box and its size, never its ink and paper, so the UA paints those from color-scheme
#slot-day | a <select>: the UA paints its own control colours, and its popup list is not stylable at all on iOS
#day-boundary | a <select>: same, and the day boundary is set from a fixed list of hours
#timer-length | a <select>: same, and it is the timer's own length in minutes
#detail-situation | a <textarea>: the app styles its box and its size, never its ink and paper
#detail-parent-kind | a <select>: what kind of place this is, chosen from a fixed list
#detail-after | a <select>: what this waits for, chosen from what already exists
#detail-merge | a <select>: which thing to fold this into
#ui-scale | a <select>: the app's own text size, chosen from a fixed list
#capacity-level | a <select>: how much you have in you, chosen from a fixed list
#pebble-weight | a <select>: how heavy this one is, chosen from a fixed list
#detail-menu-category | a <select>: which kind of wish this is
#replan-new-date | an <input type=date>: the UA owns the whole picker, calendar included
